If you haven't played the first Hotline Miami yet, I would recommend you do it before you try the sequel. In case you have played it, here are the differences:
HM2:
- Better Story;
- Longer;
- Masks more impactful;
- Ranged weapons more impactful;
HM1:
- Better Music;
- Better Atmosphere (Aesthetics?);
- Better Levels;
- Better experience overall;
While in HM1 you could easily go melee in most of the levels, HM2 makes it barely possible. Some of the levels are wide open and you will get caught often from an off screen shot. Although I think it added variety in the way you play the game (the look button became essential as well as the lock on button), you will fell often some frustration over cheap deaths. The fast paced action game on the first release was substituted for a careful, slow-paced approach on the sequel, as recklessness is now rewarded with death. The music, which is still awesome, doesn't go along well with the gameplay and that trippy atmosphere of HM1 was sort of lost.
All in all, the game is still a great experience, inferior in comparison with HM1, but perhaps as entertaining as it.

I had high hopes for this game, It could have been a nice trading game but unfortunatly the game has some problems which stop you from enjoying it.
The goal of the game is to get revenge of your father....how you do that you ask? You get richier then the guy that was responsible for your father doom, by getting an extravangant ammount of gold 1.000.000
Soo far soo good, they give you a basic tutorial but then....the first thing I notice is hmm where are the 16-bit style I was expecting? nowhere (misleading title)....well the artstyle is really good soo I let it slide.
Then the real game problems start to make themselves clear, your not trading your gambling. If you buy a maximum amount of anything, you've already failed, because then you lack the funds to travel to the next town to sell what you have.
Pros:
+Nice Artstyle.
Cons:
-Misleading Title;
-Really hard to start a new game;
-A lot of luck required;
-More like gambling not trading;
Would I recomend it?
No...has someone else on the reviews already said I would Trade the game.
